<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chick-noodle" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Chicken Noodle Soup</title>

  <p>
    Ingredients
  </p>
  <tabular>
    <row>
      <cell>2 tablespoons olive oil</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1 onion</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>3 garlic cloves</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
    <cell>5 to 6 carrots</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>3+ celery ribs</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1 teaspoon thyme</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>1 bay leaf</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>2 quarts chicken stock</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>8 ounces egg noodles</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell><m>1 \frac{1}{2}</m> pounds chicken breasts</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>Salt to taste</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

  <p>Instructions
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Gently saute onions, carrots, celery, and garlic in olive oil.  Cook patiently.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          At the same time, cook the chicken and cut into bite-size pieces.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add thyme, bay leaf, and stock to vegetables.  Simmer for 30 minutes.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add chicken to soup. Simmer 20 minutes more.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add noodles to soup. Simmer 10 to 15 minutes more.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    Notes:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          This soup does not have a lot of spices.  It gets its flavor from the vegetables (which usually fill up the pot <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> of the way raw).  The smaller you cut the vegetables, the more you get the flavor.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</section>